From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim Ansell <me@mith.ro>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 12:38:39 +0930
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] tcl/fpga: read a Lattice ECP5 TraceID

fpga/xilinx-dna.cfg reads the Device DNA a Xilinx part carries; an ECP5
has a 64-bit TraceID in the same role, read with the public UIDCODE_PUB
(0x19) instruction and a 64-bit DR scan, no ISC_ENABLE needed, as
ecpdap's read_uid() does. Bits arrive least significant first, so the
halves are assembled lo then hi with no reversal (the Xilinx DNA is the
other way round). Only the low 56 bits are factory programmed; the top 8
come from the bitstream's TRACE_ID_BINARY preference (Lattice TN1260),
so ecp5_read_traceid returns the masked value and prints both.

Refuses an all-zero or all-one shift, which is an absent or unpowered
chain rather than an identifier. Not yet exercised through an OpenOCD
adapter, which the file header says.
---
 tcl/fpga/lattice-ecp5-traceid.cfg | 23 ++++++++++++-----------
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/tcl/fpga/lattice-ecp5-traceid.cfg b/tcl/fpga/lattice-ecp5-traceid.cfg
index 2ec0089..bf7a9c2 100644
--- a/tcl/fpga/lattice-ecp5-traceid.cfg
+++ b/tcl/fpga/lattice-ecp5-traceid.cfg
@@ -4,17 +4,19 @@
 # where a Xilinx device has its Device DNA (see fpga/xilinx-dna.cfg).
 #
 # Read with UIDCODE_PUB (0x19) shifted into the 8-bit instruction register
-# and 64 bits shifted out of the data register. It is a public instruction:
-# reading it does not disturb a configured device (ecpdap's read_uid() issues
-# it with no ISC_ENABLE). Of the 64 bits the top 8 are the design's own,
-# set from the bitstream's TRACE_ID_BINARY preference; only the bottom 56
-# bits are programmed at the factory, so anything that names a board after
-# its TraceID should use the masked value this proc returns.
+# and 64 bits shifted out of the data register, the sequence ecpdap's
+# read_uid() performs (public instruction: it does not disturb a configured
+# device and needs no ISC_ENABLE). Bits arrive least significant first, so
+# the two 32-bit halves are assembled lo then hi with no bit reversal,
+# unlike the Xilinx DNA which shifts out most significant bit first.
 #
-# Sequence as in Lattice TN1260 (ECP5 sysCONFIG) and the same read as
-# rpi-hwid's fpga.py and ecpdap. Not yet checked against hardware through
-# OpenOCD: the ECP5 used to write this was reached through its Apollo
-# debug controller, not a JTAG adapter OpenOCD drives.
+# Per Lattice TN1260 (ECP5 sysCONFIG) the top 8 bits of the 64 are the
+# design's own, set from the bitstream's TRACE_ID_BINARY preference; only
+# the bottom 56 are programmed at the factory, so anything that names a
+# board after its TraceID should use the masked value this proc returns.
+#
+# Not yet exercised through an OpenOCD adapter: the ECP5 used while
+# writing this was reached through its Apollo debug controller.
 #
 #   source [find fpga/lattice_ecp5.cfg]
 #   init
@@ -22,7 +24,6 @@
 
 proc ecp5_read_traceid {tap} {
 	set UIDCODE_PUB 0x19
-	jtag arp_init
 	irscan $tap $UIDCODE_PUB
 	scan [drscan $tap 32 0 32 0] "%08x %08x" lo hi
 	set raw [expr {($hi << 32) | $lo}]
